{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of outcomes: a left-skewed lottery has a greater probability mass on favorable (positive) outcomes relative to unfavorable (negative) ones. In this case, both lotteries have a negative outcome (loss), so 'favorable' means the smaller loss (0 Euros), and 'unfavorable' means the larger loss (-6000 or -3000 Euros). A lottery is left-skewed if the probability of the smaller loss (0 Euros) is greater than that of the larger loss (negative amount).  \n\nLottery A: -3000 Euros with 0.2% probability, 0 Euros with 99.8% probability → 99.8% probability of 0 (favorable), 0.2% of -3000 (unfavorable). \nLottery B: -6000 Euros with 0.1% probability, 0 Euros with 99.9% probability → 99.9% probability of 0 (favorable), 0.1% of -6000 (unfavorable). \n\nThus, both lotteries are left-skewed, but Lottery B has a higher probability (99.9%) of the favorable outcome (0 Euros) and a lower probability (0.1%) of the unfavorable outcome (-6000). Therefore, Lottery B is more left-skewed than Lottery A. According to the decision reason, the more left-skewed lottery (B) is preferred. The relevant information for the reason is thus available and clearly derivable from the outcomes and probabilities.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Knappe Entscheidung, aber die Aussicht auf die -6000 ist sehr unatraktiv.' This translates to: 'A tight decision, but the prospect of -6000 is very unattractive.' The individual acknowledges the unattractiveness of the -6000 Euro outcome, particularly in Lottery B, which has a 0.1% chance of that loss. The individual does not explicitly mention probabilities of the 0 Euro outcome, nor does the report reference skewness, distribution, or any comparison of the shape of the outcome distributions. The justification centers on the aversion to the large loss (-6000), not on a formal evaluation of skewness or probability distribution shape. The deliberation process focuses on emotional or intuitive aversion to a specific outcome rather than a structured comparison of skewness between the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, the relative probability mass on favorable (0 Euro) versus unfavorable (negative) outcomes — and preferring the more left-skewed lottery. The individual in the report does not discuss skewness, distribution shape, or the relative probabilities of favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the justification is based solely on the unattractiveness of the -6000 Euro outcome, which reflects risk aversion or emotional discomfort, not a formal application of skewness. While the individual may have implicitly recognized that Lottery B has a more extreme negative outcome, the reasoning does not involve the formal concept of skewness or the comparison of probability distributions as required by the decision reason. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason as specified. The reasoning is qualitatively different and lacks the structural focus on skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

